  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/girardinus-uninotatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Of the currently seven accepted species of the genus Girardinus, only G. metallicus and occasionally G. falcatus are offered in the trade. G. uninotatus is not a colour miracle, but its pretty apricot colour is quite interesting.</p><p>The peaceful animals usually grow 3-5 cm long, but they grow throughout their life, so old females (which always grow larger than the males) can sometimes grow over 8 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/girardinus-uninotatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species originates from Cuba.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/girardinus-uninotatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Females always grow larger than males. Many of the males (but by no means all) develop a black spot at the base of the gonopodium (the mating organ).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/girardinus-uninotatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The name was given to this livebearer in 1860, referring to the black spot at the base of the gonopodium that many males develop.</p>"
    }
  ],
